{
  "easy": {
    "heading": "Warming Up With a Few Doubles",
    "body": "I started today's easy puzzle by looking for the biggest constraints, and that vertical column on the left really stood out. There is a region at 1,0, 2,0, and 3,0 that says all three cells must be equal. I looked at my domino pile and saw the 1,1 domino and the 3,1 domino. It made sense to put the 1,1 across 1,0 and 2,0, and then use the 1 from the 3,1 domino to fill the 3,0 spot. This immediately locked in the first few pieces.\n\nAfter that, I focused on the bottom right. The region at 3,3 has a sum target of 5. I had a 5,6 domino and a 4,5 domino left. Since the equals region at 2,4 and 3,4 needed to match the 3,3 cell, I tried using the 4,5 domino. By placing the 5 at 3,3 and the 4 at 3,4, I was able to match the 4 in the 2,4 spot using the 6,2 domino. It felt like a little chain reaction! I finished up by placing the 0,2 domino in the top right empty spots and the 5,6 domino filled in the remaining gaps at 0,3 and 0,4. It was a really satisfying way to start the morning.",
    "medium": {
    "heading": "Balancing the Equals Signs",
    "body": "The medium puzzle felt like a bit of a balancing act today! The first thing I noticed was the tiny sum region at 2,0 that had a target of 1. Since it was just one cell, I knew it had to be a 1. I looked for a domino with a 1 and found the 5,1. Placing that at 1,0 and 2,0 meant cell 1,0 was a 5. Because 1,0 and 1,1 are in an equals region, 1,1 had to be a 5 too. I paired it with the 5,2 domino, which put a 2 in cell 0,1.\n\nThat 2 in 0,1 was the key to the whole top section. Since 0,1 and 0,2 are equal, 0,2 became a 2. I used the 0,2 domino to fill 0,2 and 0,3, which put a 0 at 0,3. Then I checked the less than constraint for 0,3 and 0,4. Since 0 plus something had to be less than 4, and I had the 0,4 domino left, putting the 0 at 0,4 and the 4 at 1,4 worked perfectly. From there, the bottom right fell into place with the 2,2 domino and the 3,3 domino. It really shows how one small number like that 1 can lead you to the whole solution.",
    "hard": {
    "heading": "The Grid of Many Numbers",
    "body": "Wow, this hard puzzle from Rodolfo Kurchan was quite the workout! Unlike the others, almost every single cell had its own sum target, which basically told me exactly what number went in what spot. I spent the first few minutes just filling in the pips based on those targets, like putting a 1 at 0,0 and a 0 at 0,5. The real challenge wasn't finding the numbers, but figuring out how to connect them into the specific set of dominoes provided.\n\nI hit a bit of a snag in the middle with the 2,1 and 2,2 region that needed to sum to 9. Since the highest pip on any domino was a 5, I knew it had to be a 4 and a 5. I had to be careful because there were a few different ways to pair the 4s and 5s on the board. The breakthrough came when I realized the 2,3 and 2,4 region had to sum to 7. By process of elimination with the available dominoes like 2,3 and 1,4, I finally saw how the pairs had to sit. It was like putting together a very dense jigsaw puzzle where every piece looks similar but only fits one way.",
    "tips": "Try to find the regions with the most cells or the most restrictive rules first, like an equals region with three cells. These act as anchors for the rest of the board. Also, always keep a mental or physical list of which dominoes you have used so you do not accidentally use a 2,3 twice when you only have one.",
    "learned": "Today I learned that when a puzzle gives you a specific set of dominoes, the pool of available numbers is just as important as the grid hints. In the hard puzzle, knowing exactly which pairs were available helped me solve the sum regions much faster than just guessing numbers.\n\nI also noticed that Ian Livengood loves to use equals regions to create long paths of the same number across the board. It is a fun pattern that makes the medium puzzles feel very logical and orderly once you find the starting point.",
    "faqs": [
    {"question": "How do you solve the NYT Pips puzzle?", "answer": "You solve it by placing dominoes onto a grid so that the numbers on the dominoes match the hints in each region, such as sums or equals signs, while using every domino exactly once."},
    {"question": "What does the equals sign mean in Pips?", "answer": "An equals sign inside a outlined region means that every cell within that region must have the exact same number of pips."},
    {"question": "What are the rules for the Pips sum regions?", "answer": "A sum region contains a target number. The pips in all the cells within that outlined area must add up to exactly that target number."},
    {"question": "Can I use the same domino twice in Pips?", "answer": "No, you must use each domino from the provided list exactly once to complete the grid."},
    {"question": "What should I do if I get stuck on a Pips puzzle?", "answer": "Look for empty cells or small sum regions. These often have only one possible value, which can help you figure out which dominoes must be placed there."},
    {"question": "Is there a strategy for hard Pips puzzles?", "answer": "On harder levels, pay close attention to the list of available dominoes. Often, the numbers in the grid can only be paired in one specific way to match the dominoes you have left."}
